    -## Why it's a framework concern
    -The pattern is well known, and most teams bolt it on: add an `events` table, remember to write to
    -it in every transaction, build a poller. It works, and you carry the discipline forever. Every
    -new action has to remember. Every review has to check. The day somebody forgets, you have a
    -silent dual write, and it will not be noticed until a downstream system is measurably wrong.
    +## Why it’s a framework concern
    +The pattern is well known, and most teams bolt it on: add an `events` table, remember to write
    +to it in every transaction, build a poller. It works, and you carry the discipline forever.
    +Every new action has to remember. Every review has to check. The day somebody forgets, you have
    +a silent dual write, and it will not be noticed until a downstream system is measurably wrong.
    -at every commit. Your code stages a `DepositMoneyAction`; the executor opens the transaction,
    -writes the wallet row, writes the matching `MoneyDepositedEvent`, commits. There is no second
